Ladybird focuses the first invalid field and shows its message below
the validation anchor. Blink’s ListedElement::ShowValidationMessage
scrolls the anchor into view, focuses it or its host, and calls
UpdateVisibleValidationMessage to pass the message to the client.

WebKit’s ValidatedFormListedElement::updateVisibleValidationMessage
passes the anchor and its message to ValidationMessage. In Firefox,
HTMLFormElement::CheckValidFormSubmission collects invalid controls.
FormValidationChild focuses the field and passes its message and
position to FormValidationParent to open the popup.

On a form with an empty required email field, Chrome 158.0.8127.38,
Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 all fired the invalid event, focused
the field, and supplied a message asking the user to fill it in.

When a custom control has a hidden validation anchor, Ladybird
reports the next invalid field whose anchor can be shown. Firefox
151.0 and WebKit 26.5 also focused that next field. Chrome
158.0.8127.38 focused the custom-element host.

Ladybird suppresses the prompt if focusing hides the anchor. Chrome
158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 also focus only the first control
when its focus handler hides it and a second invalid field is visible.
